          The best way to apply teak oil is less about the oil than about what happens either side of it: clean, bright, completely dry wood going in, and every drop the wood did not absorb wiped off coming out. Get those two right and one to three coats is all most teak needs.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="before-you-start" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Before You Start: Check the Wood and the Weather
          </h2>

          <p>
            Teak oil only works when it can get into the wood. Before buying anything, find out what is already on the surface. Sprinkle a little water on the flattest part of the piece. If it darkens the wood and soaks in within a minute or so, the surface is open and will take oil. If it sits in tight beads, something is still sealing the pores, usually an old sealer, a varnish or a heavy build-up of previous oil, and new oil will sit on top of it and go gummy rather than soak in.
          </p>

          <p>
            That simple test also tells you whether oil is the finish you want at all. Oil enriches the colour and leaves the wood feeling like wood, but it carries little or no pigment, so outdoors in full sun the colour starts to fade within a season. If the honest answer is that you want the honey colour to last from spring to autumn with one application, a pigmented product is the better tool, and our <a href="/teak-oil-vs-teak-sealer/">teak oil vs teak sealer comparison</a> walks through that choice before you commit to either. For interior pieces and for people who like a quick refresh every few months, oil is the right call and the rest of this guide applies.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, look at the forecast and the thermometer. Watco's technical data sheet for its Teak Oil asks for air and surface temperatures between 55 and 90 degrees Fahrenheit on the current Canadian sheet, and Star brite's label directions for its brightener ask for temperatures above 50 degrees. In practice you want a dry, mild spell with no rain due for at least a day, and you want to work in shade. Oil applied to a surface baking in direct sun flashes off unevenly and is harder to wipe back cleanly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, gather everything before you start: a two-part teak cleaner and brightener if the wood is grey, a soft nylon scrub brush, a hose, fine sandpaper for any rough patches, the oil itself, a natural-bristle or foam brush, a stack of lint-free cotton rags, chemical-resistant gloves, and a metal can with a lid half full of water for the used rags. That last item is not optional, and the reason is covered at the end of this guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="clean" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Step 1: Clean and Brighten Grey Teak
          </h2>

          <p>
            Silver-grey teak is not dirty teak. The grey is a thin layer of weathered surface fibre where ultraviolet light has broken down the lignin that gives the wood its colour. Oil applied straight over that layer does not bring the honey colour back; it darkens the grey into a muddy, blotchy brown that looks worse than leaving it alone. The grey layer has to come off first, and on outdoor furniture the gentlest way to do that is chemical rather than mechanical.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two-part teak cleaners are the standard tool. Step one is an alkaline cleaner that lifts dirt, mildew and the loose grey fibre. Step two is an acidic brightener that neutralises the cleaner and restores a lighter, more even tone. Star brite's label directions for its Premium Teak Brightener are typical of the category: apply along the grain with a sponge, cloth or brush, rinse thoroughly with fresh water after about five minutes, use it when the temperature is above 50 degrees Fahrenheit and wear rubber gloves. Follow the specific directions on whichever brand you buy, because dwell times and dilution vary.
          </p>

          <p>
            Scrub with a soft brush along the grain, never across it, and rinse generously. Resist the pressure washer. A high-pressure jet strips out the soft summer growth between the harder grain lines, leaving a ridged, furry surface that then needs sanding to put right. A garden hose is all the rinse teak needs. If you want to understand why the two steps are sold separately, our explainer on <a href="/deck-brightener-vs-deck-cleaner/">deck brightener vs deck cleaner</a> covers the same chemistry on a larger scale.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then wait. Oil cannot penetrate wet fibres, and teak that feels dry to the touch after an hour can still be holding plenty of water inside. Give cleaned furniture at least a full day of dry weather, longer if it is humid or the pieces are thick. A good check is colour: wet teak is noticeably darker, and when the whole piece has lightened evenly to a pale honey tone it is ready. Oiling too soon is the most common reason a fresh finish goes cloudy or blotchy within a week.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wood that is simply dusty rather than grey does not need the two-part treatment. A wash with warm water and a little mild soap, a rinse and a thorough dry is enough preparation for an indoor table or a piece that was oiled last season and has only dulled slightly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sand" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Step 2: Sand Only Where It Helps, and With the Right Grit
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most teak does not need sanding before oiling, and sanding when it is not needed removes wood you cannot put back. The cleaner and brightener do the work of exposing fresh fibre chemically. Reach for abrasive only in three situations: when the grain has been raised and feels furry after years outdoors, when an old film finish is flaking and has to come off before oil can penetrate, or when you are finishing raw, newly made teak.
          </p>

          <p>
            For raw wood, Watco's technical data sheet specifies sanding unfinished surfaces with 150 to 220 grit paper and removing all the dust with a vacuum or a rag dampened with mineral spirits. That range is a sensible ceiling for teak generally. Going finer than about 220 burnishes the surface and can actually reduce how much oil it takes, which is the opposite of what you want with a penetrating finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            For raised, furry grain on weathered furniture, start one step coarser, around 120 grit, only for as long as it takes to knock the fibres flat, then finish at 150 or 180. Always sand along the grain. Cross-grain scratches that are invisible on bare wood become obvious dark lines the moment oil soaks into them. On flat table tops and wide seat slats a small random-orbit or palm sander keeps the surface flat and saves a great deal of time; our <a href="/best-palm-sander/">palm sander guide</a> covers the light-duty machines that suit furniture. Slats, legs and curved arms are faster and safer by hand with a folded sheet or a sanding sponge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dust removal is not a formality. Teak dust is fine and oily, and any left on the surface is glued into the first coat as a gritty, dull film. Vacuum with a brush attachment, then wipe with a rag lightly dampened with mineral spirits and let it flash off for a few minutes before oiling.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Situation</th>
