        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Applying teak oil well comes down to the two steps either side of the tin. Clean, bright, fully dry wood lets the oil penetrate evenly; a thorough wipe-off, checked again half an hour later, leaves a surface that feels like wood rather than a sticky film. One to three coats, following the label's own timings, is all most pieces need.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Outdoors, accept that oil is a maintenance finish and plan a light refresh every few months, or choose a pigmented sealer if that rhythm does not suit you. Indoors, oil is hard to beat. Either way, the rags go flat outside or into a sealed can of water, every time.
          </p>
